Good question — and the answer is: fix it, don't remove it. A print-friendly checklist is genuinely useful for a Certificate III field resource (learners take these into the field), and the fix is small. Removing the button loses real value.

The cleanest approach is a dedicated @media print stylesheet that hides everything except the checklist, and adds a proper heading and checkbox-friendly formatting. That way the button stays, the printout is genuinely useful, and no other part of the page is affected on screen.

Here's the fix — two small additions to your existing file.

---

1. Add this CSS block to your <style> section (near the end, before the closing </style>):

```css
/* ---------- PRINT: CHECKLIST ONLY ---------- */
@media print {
  /* Hide everything by default */
  body * {
    visibility: hidden;
  }

  /* Reveal only the checklist and its immediate container */
  .checklist,
  .checklist * {
    visibility: visible;
  }

  /* Reveal the section heading so the printout has a title */
  #field-techniques h2,
  #field-techniques h3 {
    visibility: visible;
  }

  /* Position the checklist at the top-left of the page */
  .checklist {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    width: 100%;
    display: block;
    grid-template-columns: none;
    gap: 0;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* Print-friendly title above the list */
  .checklist::before {
    content: "Field Kit Checklist — CIII CEM";
    display: block;
    font-size: 1.4rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: #1e3a1e;
    border-bottom: 3px solid #e6b422;
    padding-bottom: 0.4rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.8rem;
    visibility: visible;
  }

  /* Each item as a clean line with a visible empty box */
  .checklist-item {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    padding: 0.35rem 0;
    background: none;
    border-bottom: 1px dotted #bbb;
    page-break-inside: avoid;
  }

  .checklist-item input[type="checkbox"] {
    width: 14px;
    height: 14px;
    accent-color: #000;
  }

  .checklist-item label {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: #000;
  }

  /* Hide the print button itself and any other UI chrome */
  .print-btn,
  .nav-wrapper,
  .hero,
  footer {
    display: none !important;
  }

  /* Prevent awkward page breaks */
  section {
    page-break-inside: auto;
  }
}
```

---

2. Change the print button to use the browser's print dialog (no change needed to the onclick, but I'd recommend this small tweak):

Your current button is:

```html
<button class="print-btn" onclick="window.print()"><i class="fas fa-print"></i> Print Checklist</button>
```

That's already correct — window.print() triggers the print dialog, and the @media print block above filters what actually prints. No JS change needed. If you'd like, you can add a small comment in the HTML so future-you remembers why it works:

```html
<!-- Print Checklist: see @media print block in <style> — only the checklist is output -->
<button class="print-btn" onclick="window.print()">
  <i class="fas fa-print"></i> Print Checklist
</button>
```

---

Why this approach (over commenting out the button)

· Learners get a real field resource. A clean checklist with empty boxes and dotted writing lines is exactly the kind of thing a CIII student would take outdoors. The whole point of a checklist is to be printed — hiding that defeats its purpose.
· No side effects on screen. The @media print rules only fire when printing, so your beautiful on-screen layout is untouched.
· Fully reversible and self-documenting. If you ever change the checklist HTML, only the @media print block needs updating — nothing else in the page cares.
· Bonus heading included. The ::before pseudo-element adds a title ("Field Kit Checklist — CIII CEM") that appears only on the printed page, so the printout is self-contained and looks intentional rather than like a stray fragment of a web page.

If you'd prefer the printed version to include a small page footer (e.g. "© 2025 Jay Rowley · CIII CEM"), that's one more line inside the @media print block — say the word and I'll add it.
